sub(p,'Corrections to the MST architecture')
text(p,'MST slides 3 and 7 mention Spring/Hibernate, React, REST controllers and interchangeable server databases. Those technologies are not dependencies or runtime components of the inspected application. The final architecture is a JavaFX desktop process with ordinary Java method calls and JDBC DAOs. There are no FXML screen files despite the javafx-fxml dependency.',
'Slide 2 lists multi-user accounts out of scope, while slide 5 requires registration and sign-in. The final system resolves this tension by implementing multiple local accounts with one current session. It does not implement simultaneous remote multi-user service access. Slide 6 treats a database and automated scheduler as secondary actors; the final use-case boundary treats both as internal components.',
'The presentation groups functions under FR-01 through FR-08. Source comments use a different, more granular FR numbering, for example budgets as FR-06/07 and recommendations as FR-13. This report retains the original eight MST identifiers as its requirement baseline and records comment aliases explicitly. Additional documentation IDs EXT-01 through EXT-04 identify final features without silently renumbering the MST.')
sub(p,'Constraints')
text(p,'The application depends on a compatible JavaFX runtime, available local files and a writable working directory. Recurring work does not execute while the process is closed; startup catch-up handles overdue occurrences. No response-time benchmark, concurrency capacity or formal security certification is claimed.')
src(p,'pom.xml','service/AuthService.java','app/MainApp.java','Finance_Analyzer_MST.pptx:slides 2,3,5,6,7')

p=page('2. ANALYSIS PHASE','2.1 Use Cases',1);sub(p,'2.1 Use Cases');sub(p,'2.1.1 Use-Case Diagrams')
text(p,'The primary actor is the account holder. Before sign-in, the same person may create an account or submit credentials. After sign-in, the account identifier is passed to the controllers. Diagram boundaries represent the entire local application; SQLite and the recurring scheduler are not external actors.')
fig(p,'01_usecase_track','Account access and transaction tracking',380)
text(p,'Recording an entry includes validation; editing also validates the changed data. Deletion is confirmed rather than treated as a validation subcase. Searching and category creation are separate user goals. A successful account registration opens the same application shell as a successful login. No administrator or password-reset workflow is modeled because the source does not expose those capabilities.')
src(p,'ui/controllers/LoginController.java:handleAction()','ui/controllers/TransactionsController.java','service/TransactionService.java')

p=page('Use-case model continued','2.1.1 Planning and analysis')
fig(p,'02_usecase_plan','Planning, automation and analysis use cases',390)
text(p,'The planning use cases represent persisted budgets and goals. Creating or activating a goal checks the sum of active allocation ratios. Allocating a deposit is a separate operation; creating income does not automatically allocate savings. Managing a recurring rule configures future ledger generation rather than instructing a bank to debit funds.',
'Reports, charts and advice read saved records. The advice toggle and fixed-category selection are distinct preferences: disabling advice suppresses all rule messages; marking a category fixed excludes it only from discretionary concentration and category-spike checks. Snapshot creation and restore are exposed functions, but restore is partial and is not a complete account recovery contract.')
src(p,'service/GoalService.java:createGoal(),toggleGoalActive()','service/RatioAllocationService.java:allocateDeposit()','io/BackupManager.java:restoreBackup()')

p=page('2.1.2 Use Case Templates','2.1.2 Account access')
uc(p,'UC-01','Register, sign in and sign out','FR-01','Application initialization completes; local database is reachable.','Select Register & Sign In or Sign In.','1. Enter username and password. 2. Validate registration fields or nonempty login input. 3. Registration checks duplicate username, hashes the password and saves User; login retrieves User and checks BCrypt. 4. Initialize default fixed-category preferences as needed. 5. Set currentUser and open the shell. 6. Sign Out clears currentUser and shows login.','Invalid fields or credentials produce an AuthenticationException message. Duplicate usernames are rejected. Database failures are not comprehensively caught by the login handler, so they remain a reliability risk. Canceling registration mode does not create an account.','A successful operation establishes one in-memory session. Password plaintext is not saved; logout does not delete stored records.','service/AuthService.java:register(),login(),logout(); dao/jdbc/JdbcUserDao.java:save()')
text(p,'Registration accepts at least six username characters from letters, digits, underscore, dot and hyphen; demo is a special accepted short name. Password validation requires at least six characters. These are implemented rules, not a recommendation that six-character passwords are sufficient for production security. Account deletion and credential recovery are not user-facing functions.')

p=page('Transaction use-case templates','2.1.2 Transaction maintenance')
uc(p,'UC-02','Record income or expense','FR-02, FR-07','Signed in; an appropriate category is available.','Transactions > New Transaction.','1. Choose Income or Expense. 2. Enter amount, category, date, payment mode and optional notes. 3. Observe live preview. 4. Save. 5. UI and service validation run. 6. Income goes through transactionDao.save(); expense uses direct JDBC in addExpense(). 7. Table reloads; dashboard/budgets reload on navigation.','Nonpositive amounts and scale above two decimal places are rejected. Expense dates after today are rejected. SQL insert failure reports an error; addExpense rolls back. Preview does not itself save any data.','A new persisted row has a generated ID, positive stored amount and explicit INCOME/EXPENSE type.','ui/controllers/TransactionDialogController.java:handleSave(); service/TransactionService.java:addIncome(),addExpense()')
uc(p,'UC-03','Edit or delete a transaction','FR-02','Signed in; selected row belongs to current account.','Double-click row to edit or choose Delete Transaction.','Edit: open populated dialog, keep the type locked, change permitted fields, save and reload. Delete: request deletion, confirm, call deleteTransaction(id,userId), then reload.','Cancel closes without persistence. No selection produces a prompt. Failed updates/deletes may return false or raise a data-access exception.','Successful edit replaces stored values; successful delete removes the selected user-scoped row.','ui/controllers/TransactionsController.java:handleEditSelected(),handleDeleteSelected(); dao/jdbc/JdbcTransactionDao.java:update(),deleteById()')

p=page('Category and budget use-case templates','2.1.2 Categories and budgets')
uc(p,'UC-04','Classify and find financial records','FR-02, FR-03','Signed in; transaction list initialized.','Add Category or apply Search & Filters.','Create a named category with income/expense type; reject an existing case-insensitive name. For retrieval, specify date range, category, type, amount bounds or notes/category keyword; execute user-scoped query and replace table data. Reset removes filters.','Blank or duplicate category names are rejected. Nonnumeric filter amounts currently become null rather than producing validation feedback. Custom category classification does not alter existing rows automatically.','A saved category becomes selectable; filtered results remain scoped to the account.','service/TransactionService.java:addCustomCategory(),findFiltered(); ui/controllers/TransactionsController.java:applyFilters()')
uc(p,'UC-05','Manage monthly category budgets','FR-04','Signed in; expense category and month selected.','Budgets > Set Category Budget.','Enter a positive limit and save. Upsert the unique user/category/month record. Query spending in that category and month. Calculate utilization and show NORMAL, WARNING or EXCEEDED. Edit or delete through the row menu.','Zero/negative limits fail validation. Missing budget gives normal status in the alert service. Overspending is allowed; warnings do not block saving an expense.','A persisted monthly limit and calculated spending comparison are visible.','service/BudgetService.java:setBudget(),calculateUtilisation(); dao/jdbc/JdbcBudgetDao.java:save()')

p=page('Savings use-case template','2.1.2 Goal creation and allocation')
uc(p,'UC-06','Create, manage and fund savings goals','FR-05, EXT-04','Signed in; current active-goal ratios are available.','Savings Goals > New Savings Goal or Allocate Savings Deposit.','1. Enter title, positive target, deadline and ratio from 0 to 100. 2. Validate fields and active ratio sum. 3. Save a new active goal. 4. Enter a positive deposit. 5. Read active goals and calculate each rounded share. 6. Batch saved_paise increments within one JDBC transaction. 7. Commit and show allocation summary.','Blank titles, nonpositive targets, missing/past deadlines or out-of-range ratios are rejected. The implementation accepts today as a deadline. Exceeding 100% active allocation raises AllocationRatioException. SQL failure rolls back allocation updates. Cancel performs no write.','Goal rows reflect saved allocations. Remainder is returned in the result but has no persisted unallocated-savings account.','service/GoalService.java:createGoal(),updateGoal(),toggleGoalActive(); service/RatioAllocationService.java:allocateDeposit()')
text(p,'Example expectation, not an executed database test: a INR 1,000 deposit with active ratios 60% and 30% yields INR 600 and INR 300, with INR 100 unallocated. Allocation does not reduce the transaction net cashflow. Progress display is capped at 100%, although saved amounts may exceed the target.',
'The New/Edit dialog uses a scrollable form and separate action footer. Its size is capped to the current screen bounds after showing. This code change addresses clipping; 100%, 125% and 150% scaling still require manual acceptance checks.')

p=page('Recurring use-case template','2.1.2 Recurring entries')
uc(p,'UC-07','Configure and process recurring transactions','FR-06','Signed in to create rules; application process running to execute scheduled work.','Create Rule, Run Catch-up Now, startup catch-up or periodic timer.','1. Select type/category, positive amount, frequency and start date; end date is optional. 2. Save ACTIVE rule with nextDueDate=startDate. 3. Find active rules due on/before today. 4. Generate each missed occurrence. 5. Save audit message and advance date with Frequency.nextDate(). 6. Mark expired when the following date exceeds the end date.','End date before start is rejected. Paused rules are skipped until resumed. Processing failure is logged. The current save/audit/advance sequence is not one atomic database transaction, so a mid-sequence failure may cause duplicate retry records.','Transactions carry recurring_rule_id; rule stores its next date/status; execution history records success/failure.','ui/controllers/RecurringController.java:handleAddRule(),handleRunCatchUp(); service/RecurringScheduler.java:runCatchUp(),processRule()')
table(p,'Recurring intervals and persisted state',['Item','Implemented meaning'],[['DAILY / WEEKLY','Advance using LocalDate.plusDays(1) / plusWeeks(1).'],['MONTHLY / YEARLY','Advance from the current occurrence using plusMonths(1) / plusYears(1).'],['ACTIVE / PAUSED / EXPIRED','Persisted RuleStatus enum values. No background service runs when app is closed.']],[.31,.69])
text(p,'The manual catch-up command calls the scheduler synchronously from the UI handler. Large backlogs can block interaction; no backlog performance measurement was performed. Automatic periodic callbacks use Platform.runLater to refresh UI tables.')

p=page('Analysis and file use-case templates','2.1.2 Reports and data transfer')
uc(p,'UC-08','Review financial position and preferences','FR-08, EXT-01, EXT-03','Signed in; saved records may be empty.','Open Dashboard or refresh monthly report; change advice/fixed preferences.','Read income, expense and category totals. Construct charts and goal snapshot. Build AnalysisContext and evaluate five rules when enabled. Generate monthly text with category/budget/goal summaries. Persist preference changes and refresh insight display after successful advice update.','Zero income avoids division by zero. Disabled advice produces no messages. Individual rule exceptions are logged and do not stop all other rules. Historical micro-spend advice is not strictly aligned to the selected report month.','A read-only analysis is displayed; only explicitly changed preferences are saved.','ui/controllers/DashboardController.java:refreshView(); service/ReportService.java:generateMonthlyReportText(); service/HeuristicEngine.java:generateRecommendations()')
uc(p,'UC-09','Transfer financial data through files','EXT-02','Signed in; suitable file path selected.','Reports & Settings file button.','Export writes CSV or text; import validates CSV rows and counts imported/skipped records. Backup serializes UserDataSnapshot. Restore reads snapshot and appends selected records to the current account.','Canceling file selection performs no operation. Invalid rows are skipped with reasons. Restore omits recurring rules/preferences, may duplicate records and is not atomic as a whole.','Selected output file exists after successful export; imported records persist where accepted. Restore is only a partial import, not exact recovery.','io/CsvImporter.java; io/CsvExporter.java; io/BackupManager.java')

p=page('2.2 Activity Diagram and Swimlane Diagrams','2.2 Expense activity')
fig(p,'03_activity_expense','Expense entry with validation and persistence',355)
text(p,'The activity starts when the account holder opens a new transaction dialog. The amount and category listeners calculate an advisory preview while the record remains unsaved. Save triggers explicit field parsing and validation. The expense service checks the selected category and rejects an invalid amount or future date.',
'The expense insertion is protected by setAutoCommit(false), commit and rollback. Generated keys populate the object identifier. Only the success path closes the dialog and refreshes the transaction table. Returning to Dashboard or Budgets calls the navigation refresh callbacks. These activities do not imply a bank transaction or automatic savings-goal deduction.')
src(p,'ui/controllers/TransactionDialogController.java:triggerPreviewUpdate(),handleSave()','service/TransactionService.java:addExpense()')

p=page('Savings activity','2.2 Goal activity')
fig(p,'04_activity_savings','Goal creation followed by ratio allocation',340)
text(p,'Goal creation and funding are separate actions. Validation checks the new ratio against the total of currently active goals. A new goal begins active with zero saved amount. A deposit then uses the current active set to calculate shares and applies all saved-balance changes inside a JDBC transaction.',
'An inactive goal receives no new allocation. The remainder reported to the user is a calculated result, not an account balance in the schema. Independent per-goal rounding can over-allocate tiny deposits; this is a known limitation rather than an omitted decision state. A target being reached is not an automatic transition to inactive: active status is explicitly changed by the user.')
src(p,'service/GoalService.java:createGoal()','service/RatioAllocationService.java:allocateDeposit()','model/SavingsGoal.java:getProgressPercentage()')

p=page('Expense swimlane diagram','2.2 Responsibility allocation')
fig(p,'05_swimlane_expense','Responsibilities across user, interface, logic and persistence',350)
text(p,'The swimlanes separate responsibility rather than imply remote services. The user supplies information; JavaFX controls collect it; the transaction service enforces validation; JDBC reads categories and inserts the expense. These operations occur in the local process. There is no HTTP request, REST controller, ORM session or server-side deployment.',
'The diagram intentionally shows direct expense SQL under persistence while naming TransactionService as its caller. addExpense() contains that SQL itself; it does not delegate to JdbcTransactionDao.save(). Income creation does use the DAO save method. Acknowledging this asymmetry is more accurate than presenting a perfectly uniform architecture that the code does not implement.')
src(p,'service/TransactionService.java:addIncome(),addExpense()','ui/controllers/TransactionsController.java:handleAddTransaction()')

p=page('2.3 Data Flow Diagrams','2.3.1 DFD Level 0');sub(p,'2.3.1 DFD Level 0')
fig(p,'06_dfd_context','Context diagram with external entities and data flows',365)
text(p,'The context boundary includes authentication, financial records, analytics, scheduled processing and persistence. The external account holder exchanges credentials, entry data, plan settings, queries and preferences with the application. A user-selected file is a separate external source/sink for CSV, report text and serialized snapshots.',
'DFD notation is data-oriented: rectangles are external entities, circles are transformations, arrows carry named data, and parallel-rule stores appear only in decomposed levels. An arrow is not a method call or execution-order edge. The context diagram deliberately excludes a database actor because SQLite is inside the system boundary.',
'The aggregated user-input flow is decomposed at Level 1 into credentials, tracking details, planning settings, analysis queries/preferences and file-operation queries. Outputs are decomposed into authentication/operation results, record views, previews, charts/advice and reports. File inputs and outputs retain the same logical content across levels.')
src(p,'app/MainApp.java:showMainShell()','ui/controllers/ReportsSettingsController.java','io/UserDataSnapshot.java')

p=page('2.3.2 DFD Level 1','2.3.2 DFD Level 1')
fig(p,'07_dfd_level1','Major transformations and logical stores',440)
text(p,'Processes 1.0-5.0 divide authentication, tracking, planning, analysis/reporting and file transfer. D1 groups account/preference tables; D2 groups category, transaction and recurring data; D3 groups budgets and savings goals. Repeated symbols identify the same external account holder and logical stores, not extra users or databases.',
'The same account-holder and file exchanges from Level 0 remain at Level 1. File-operation query is a decomposition of the context query input. Internal report text passes from analysis to transfer for text export. Internal scheduled generation belongs to tracking and is not represented as a new external user input. DFDs describe the implemented data responsibilities, including partial restore; they do not assert atomicity of all process writes.')
src(p,'dao/jdbc/DatabaseManager.java:initializeDatabase()','service/ReportService.java','io/BackupManager.java')

p=page('2.3.3 DFD Level 2','2.3.3 DFD Level 2')
fig(p,'08_dfd_level2','Decomposition of tracking process 2.0',330)
text(p,'This diagram expands only process 2.0. Transaction details enter validation, valid entries reach persistence, queries reach retrieval, and category/rule settings reach their management transformation. The D2 interface retains stored-record reads and record-change writes. The external outputs remain operation results and record views.',
'A validation result is one kind of operation result, not an additional context-level output. The diagram does not add budget-preview inputs to process 2.0 because previews belong to analysis process 4.0 at Level 1. This separation keeps the parent and child interfaces balanced. In the actual UI both responsibilities are combined in the transaction dialog.',
'The official contents repeat number 2.3.2 for Level 2. The numbering here is corrected to 2.3.3 while preserving the required hierarchy and all three DFD levels.')
